🧩 First, the Core Idea

React re-renders components whenever their state or props change.
During re-render, functions and objects get recreated — even if their logic or value hasn’t changed.

That’s wasteful in some cases (especially in large components or child re-renders).
So React gives us memoization hooks — like useMemo() and useCallback() — to cache results or functions.

🧠 Analogy: The “Chef and Recipe” Analogy

Imagine you’re a chef (React component) cooking dishes (UI output).

🥣 Every time someone orders, you redo all steps from scratch (re-render).

🧂 But some dishes take a lot of time — like making sauce.

🧊 So you can store (memoize) the sauce — reuse it next time if the ingredients are the same!

👉 That’s useMemo:

“Don’t remake the sauce (expensive value) unless ingredients change.”

Now, if you have a helper (function) to cut vegetables — React recreates it every order.
But if you memoize that helper...

👉 That’s useCallback:

“Don’t recreate the helper (function) unless the ingredients (dependencies) change.”

---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
⚡ useMemo — Cache EXPENSIVE VALUES

🧩 Purpose:

Caches the result of a computation so it isn’t recalculated unnecessarily on every render.

📘 Syntax:

const memoizedValue = useMemo(() => computeSomething(a, b), [a, b]);

⚙️ Example:
function ProductList({ products, filter }) {
  // Expensive filtering operation
  const filteredProducts = useMemo(() => {
    console.log("Filtering products...");
    return products.filter(p => p.category === filter);
  }, [products, filter]); // Only recalculate when these change

  return (
    <ul>
      {filteredProducts.map(p => <li key={p.id}>{p.name}</li>)}
    </ul>
  );
}
✅ Use case:

* You’re doing expensive calculations (filtering, sorting, mapping large arrays).

* You want to avoid recalculating unless inputs change.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
⚡ useCallback — Cache FUNCTION REFERENCES
🧩 Purpose:

Caches a function so it doesn’t get recreated every render.

📘 Syntax:
const memoizedFn = useCallback(() => doSomething(a, b), [a, b]);

⚙️ Example:
function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Function to pass to a child
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    setCount(c => c + 1);
  }, []);

  return <Child onClick={handleClick} />;
}

const Child = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {
  console.log("Child re-rendered");
  return <button onClick={onClick}>Click</button>;
});

✅ Use case:

* You’re passing callbacks to child components (especially those wrapped in React.memo).

* You want to prevent unnecessary child re-renders due to new function references.

🧠 Analogy:

Instead of teaching your helper a new cutting technique every time,
you reuse the same technique — unless ingredients change.
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
⚡ React.useMemo vs useMemo

They are exactly the same — just different ways to access it.

✅ useMemo → imported directly
✅ React.useMemo → accessed through the React namespace

Example:

import React, { useMemo } from "react";

// SAME as:
React.useMemo(() => {...}, [deps]);

⚙️ Use useMemo (imported version) — it’s cleaner and more common.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------
🧩 When to Use Each — Quick Summary Table
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Hook            | What it caches         | When to use                                       | Analogy                 |
| --------------- | ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------- |
| `useMemo`       | **Value (result)**     | Expensive calculations (filtering, sorting, etc.) | Save pre-made sauce     |
| `useCallback`   | **Function reference** | Pass function to memoized child or event          | Save helper’s technique |
| `React.useMemo` | Same as `useMemo`      | Rarely used — just a namespace variant            | Same thing              |
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

⚡ Combined Example (Real World)


import React, { useState, useMemo, useCallback } from "react";

function App() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState("All");

  const products = [
    { id: 1, name: "Apple", category: "Fruit" },
    { id: 2, name: "Carrot", category: "Vegetable" },
    { id: 3, name: "Mango", category: "Fruit" }
  ];

  // Expensive filtering
  const filteredProducts = useMemo(() => {
    console.log("Filtering...");
    return filter === "All"
      ? products
      : products.filter(p => p.category === filter);
  }, [filter]);

  // Memoized event handler
  const handleIncrement = useCallback(() => {
    setCount(c => c + 1);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Count: {count}</h1>
      <button onClick={handleIncrement}>Increment</button>

      <select onChange={e => setFilter(e.target.value)}>
        <option>All</option>
        <option>Fruit</option>
        <option>Vegetable</option>
      </select>

      <ul>
        {filteredProducts.map(p => (
          <li key={p.id}>{p.name}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>
  );
}

✅ useMemo → prevents re-filtering unless filter changes
✅ useCallback → prevents re-creating handleIncrement every render

⚠️ When NOT to Use

* Don’t use useMemo or useCallback everywhere — it can make code complex.

Use them only when:

* You notice performance issues, or

* You’re passing functions to memoized children.

=================================================================================
* useCallback() 👉 Memoizes functions (like event handlers).

* React.memo() 👉 Memoizes components (prevents unnecessary re-renders).

⚡ Why we need both together
Problem:

In React, every re-render creates a new function — even if logic is same.
So when a parent component passes that function as a prop to a child component:

<Child onClick={handleClick} />

React sees:
“Hey, onClick is a new function! Child must re-render.”

Even if the function does exactly the same thing.
That’s wasted work 🥱.

⚙️ Solution: useCallback() + React.memo()

To stop that, we do this:

1. useCallback() → tells React “keep the same function reference unless dependencies change.”

2. React.memo() → tells React “don’t re-render this component unless its props actually changed.”

Together:

The child will not re-render when parent re-renders — unless the actual props change.

💡 Analogy

Imagine:

* Parent (Boss) gives instructions (functions) to Employee (Child).

* Every time boss speaks, employee listens again — even if it’s the same instruction! 😅

So:

🧠 useCallback() = Boss writes the instruction down and reuses the same note each time.

🧊 React.memo() = Employee checks: “Is the note the same as last time? If yes, I won’t repeat the work.”


✅ With useCallback() + React.memo()

import React, { useState, useCallback } from "react";

const Child = React.memo(({ onClick }) => {
  console.log("Child rendered");
  return <button onClick={onClick}>Child Button</button>;
});

export default function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Memoized function
  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log("Clicked");
  }, []); // no dependency — so it stays same reference

  console.log("Parent rendered");

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increase Count</button>
      <Child onClick={handleClick} />
    </>
  );
}

🧩 Now what happens:

Parent re-renders when count changes ✅

handleClick stays the same reference 🧠

React.memo() compares old/new props — they’re identical 🚫

Child does not re-render 💪
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
| Concept         | Level           | Purpose                             | Typical Use                           |
| --------------- | --------------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| `useCallback()` | Function level  | Memoize event handlers or callbacks | `onClick`, `onChange`, etc.           |
| `React.memo()`  | Component level | Memoize functional components       | Child components that depend on props |
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

✅ When you pass a function prop from parent → child,
➡️ wrap the child with React.memo(), and
➡️ wrap the function with useCallback().

That’s the winning combo.
------------------------------------------------------------------------
⚠️ Common Mistakes to Avoid

❌ Using useCallback() without React.memo()
→ No benefit — because the child will re-render anyway.

❌ Wrapping everything in useCallback()
→ Adds unnecessary complexity and memory overhead.

✅ Use it only when:

* You’re passing functions to memoized child components, or

* The function creation is expensive.


